  <title> Worksheet 24 (LN 2.2, 2.3) </title>
    <page xml:id="ws24-poll-question">
        <title> Poll Question </title> 
        <p> True or False: <m> Q(x,y) = 8x^2 +2y^2 - 8xy \gt 0 </m> for all values of <m> x </m> and <m> y </m> </p>
        <p> <em> Hint: plot it in Desmos 3D! And: how does this exercise relate to symmetric matrices? </em> </p>
    
       <me> ======================= </me>     
    </page>

    

    <page xml:id="ws24-quadratic-forms">
        <p> <term> 24.1 Expressing Quadratic Forms </term> </p> 
         <definition> 
            <p>
                <ul> 
            <li>
              <p>
                A quadratic form is a function: <m> 𝑄 :ℝ𝑛 →ℝ </m> given by,
              </p>
            </li>

        </ul> 

        <me>
          Q(\vec{x}) = \vec{x}^T A \vec{x} =
          \begin{pmatrix}
            x_1 \amp x_2 \amp \ldots \amp x_n
          \end{pmatrix}
          \begin{pmatrix}
            a_{11} \amp a_{12} \amp \ldots \amp a_{1n} \\
            a_{12} \amp a_{22} \amp \ldots \amp a_{2n} \\
            \vdots \amp \vdots \amp \ddots \amp \vdots \\
            a_{1n} \amp a_{2n} \amp \ldots \amp a_{nn}
          \end{pmatrix}
          \begin{pmatrix}
            x_1 \\
            x_2 \\
            \vdots \\
            x_n
          \end{pmatrix}
        </me>

        <ul>
          <li> 
            Matrix A is <m> n </m> x <m> n </m> and symmetric, and <m> \vec{x} </m> is a variable vector in <m> ℝ^n. </m>
          </li> 
          
        </ul>
         
            </p> 
        </definition> 
        

        <p> Example: express <m> Q = 3x_1 ^ 2 + x_2 ^ 2 + 8x_3 ^ 3 + 4x_1x_2 - 6x_2x_3 </m> in the form <m> Q = x^TAx</m>  </p>
    
            <me> ======================= </me>   
    </page>

    <page xml:id="ws24-principal-axes">
      <p> <term> 24.2 The Principle Axes Theorem </term> </p>
        <theorem> <p>If <m> A </m> is a symmetric matrix then there exists an orthogonal change of variable <m> \vec{x} = P\vec{y} </m>   
            that transforms <m> \vec{x}^TA\vec{x} </m> to <m> \vec{y}^T D\vec{y} </m> with no cross-product terms. </p> </theorem> 
       

       <p> <em> Proof in the lecture videos/textbook </em> </p> 
       
       <p> <term> 24.2.1 Example</term> </p>
       <p> Consider <m> Q = x^2 + y^2 + 2kxy </m> where <m> k ∈ ℝ. </m> </p>
        <p> 
          <ol> 
            <li> 
              Make a change of variable that transforms the quadratic form into another quadratic that has no cross-product terms.
            </li>

            <li> 
                For what values of <m> k </m> will <m> Q </m> satisfy <m> Q \geq 0 </m> for any <m> x </m> and <m> y </m>?
            </li>

          </ol>
        </p>  


        <p> <em> Tip: it may help to plot the surfaces in Desmos 3D to see what the surface looks like as you change value of <m> k </m> </em> </p> 
    
            <me> ======================= </me>  
    
    </page>

    <page xml:id="ws24-classifying-surfaces">
      <p> <term> 24.3 Classifying Quadratic Surfaces </term> </p>
      <p> Our goal: <term> solve optimization problems with quadratic functions. </term> </p>
      <p> Take for example the surface: </p> 
      <me>
          Q = x^2 + y^2 = \vec{x}^T
          \begin{pmatrix}
            1 \amp 0 \\
            0 \amp 1
          \end{pmatrix}
          \vec{x}
      </me> 
      <p> If we set <m> Q </m> to be constant, <m> Q = k^2, </m> we obtain the equation of a circle whose radius is <m> k^2. </m> </p>
      <me> 
      k^2 = x^2 + y^2
      </me>

       <image label="quadratic-24" width="30%">
              
              <latex-image>
                \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1]
        \begin{axis}[
            axis line style = very thick, smooth, 
            xmin=-4,ymin=-4,zmin=0,xmax=4,ymax=4,zmax=10,
            axis lines=center,
            xticklabels={,,}, yticklabels={,,},zticklabels={,,},
            xtick=\empty, ytick=\empty, ztick=\empty, 
            % xlabel = $x$,ylabel=$y$,zlabel=$z$
            ]
        \addplot3 [surf,shader=flat,draw=black,draw=none,restrict z to domain=0:9, data cs=polar, domain=0:360, y domain=0:3] (x, y, y^2);
    \end{axis}    
    \end{tikzpicture}  
              </latex-image>
          </image>

    <p> The surface above has a unique minimum at the origin. What about other surfaces? </p>

        <definition> <p> A Quadratic Form Q is </p> 
            <ul>
                <li> <term> positive definite </term> if <m> Q  &gt; 0 </m> for all <m> \vec{x} \neq \vec{0} </m>  </li>
                <li> <term> negative definite </term> if  <m> Q  \lt  0 </m> for all <m> \vec{x} \neq \vec{0} </m> </li>
                <li> <term> positive semidefinite </term> if <m> Q  \geq  0 </m> for all <m> \vec{x} </m> </li>
                <li> <term> negative semidefinite </term> if <m> Q  \leq  0 </m> for all <m> \vec{x} </m> </li>    
                <li> <term> indefinite </term> if <m> Q </m> takes on positive and negative values for <m> \vec{x} \neq \vec{0} </m> </li>    
            </ul>
        </definition>


        

       
        <theorem> <p> If <m> A </m>  is a symmetric matrix with eigenvalues <m> λ_i </m> , then <m> Q = \vec{x}^TA\vec{x} </m> is</p> 
            <ul>
                <li> <term> positive definite </term>  when all eigenvalues are positive </li>
                <li> <term> negative definite </term> when all eigenvalues are negative</li>
                <li> <term>indefinite  </term> when at least one eigenvalue is negative and at least one eigenvalue is positive </li>
            
            </ul>
        </theorem> 

        <me> ======================= </me>  
        </page> 

